<GuideLayout guide={guides[2]} heading={guides[2].name}>
  <div class="reference-answer"><p>Measure I taxes purchases and helps pay for general City services. Measure PFD would tax property based on building square footage and pay for specified fire and emergency-response needs. Compare what each tax applies to, how long it lasts, and how the money can be spent below.</p></div>
  <section id="comparison"><h2>Compare the taxes side by side</h2><p class="table-scroll-hint">Scroll horizontally to compare both measures.</p><div class="reference-table-wrap" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Comparison table, scroll horizontally on smaller screens"><table class="reference-table comparison"><caption>Measure I’s 2018 ballot description compared with Measure PFD’s proposed ordinance.</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Feature</th><th scope="col">Measure I</th><th scope="col">Measure PFD</th></tr></thead><tbody>
  <tr><th scope="row">Status</th><td>Approved by voters in November 2018.</td><td>On the November 3, 2026 ballot.</td></tr>
  <tr><th scope="row">What is taxed, and how much?</th><td>A three-quarter-cent sales tax, equivalent to 0.75% of taxable purchases.</td><td>$0.19 per square foot of improved property annually, subject to exemptions.</td></tr>
  <tr><th scope="row">How long does it last?</th><td>Until ended by voters, according to the ballot question.</td><td>14 years after its effective date, unless extended by voters.</td></tr>
  <tr><th scope="row">What can it pay for?</th><td>General City services, including fire, police, streets, homelessness services, and other listed purposes.</td><td>Specified fire facilities, equipment, emergency-response enhancements, and administration.</td></tr>
  <tr><th scope="row">How is spending checked?</th><td>The ballot question requires audits and local control.</td><td>Annual independent audit, annual public report, and City Council oversight specified in the ordinance.</td></tr>
  </tbody></table></div></section>
  <section id="fire-funding"><h2>Does Measure I support fire services?</h2><p>Yes. Fire and paramedic services and keeping fire stations open were among the purposes named in Measure I’s ballot question. That question also listed other general-fund services. Measure I was never limited to fire stations.</p><p>The accompanying advisory question asked about using two-thirds of the annual revenue for City services and one-third for public schools. That advisory question differs from PFD’s proposed rules limiting how its money can be spent.</p><p>Measure PFD names reconstruction of Stations 33 and 37 within the first ten years among its purposes. Whether existing revenues should instead pay for those projects is a budget-policy question; the comparison alone does not establish that a particular project has already been funded.</p><p><a href="/measure-pfd-fire-station-funding/">See what PFD would fund</a>.</p></section>
  <section id="campaign-view"><h2>Why we oppose Measure PFD</h2><p>We believe City Hall should use existing resources to fund fire facilities before asking you to pay another property tax.</p><p><a href="/bill-paparian/">Read why Bill Paparian opposes PFD</a> and <a href="/facts/#measure">our fact sheet</a>.</p></section>
</GuideLayout>
